  "findings": [
    "At 120 Hz / 1000 Mbps / 4:2:0, a bounded wait that runs only while a frame is decoding raised displayed target FPS in both sessions: 108.3 -> 114.7 / 116.1 (1500 / 3000 us) and 107.2 -> 110.1 / 111.4 / 113.2 (2000 / 3000 / 4000 us).",
    "4000 us beat no wait in 10 of 10 paired cycles (mean +6.0 FPS); remaining loss is mostly frames whose packets had not arrived, which the wait deliberately ignores.",
    "Estimated pipeline latency is quantized to display periods and drifts between states over tens of seconds independent of the wait; no latency difference attributable to the wait was observed.",
    "Ready-FD early publication lowered displayed FPS in every comparison (108.3 -> 87.2 without wait) and pushed sessions into the higher vsync-queue state.",
    "At the saved 144 Hz / 2000 Mbps / 4:4:4 settings, decode took 16-19 ms per frame: about 74 frames/s were displayed and about 70/s lost; the wait (capped at 3472 us) was neutral.",
    "On .31 the unset default (logged as 4000 us) beat an explicit 0 in 10 of 10 paired cycles at 120 Hz / 1000 Mbps / 4:2:0: 110.4 -> 114.5 displayed FPS.",
    "At 144 Hz / 1000 Mbps / 4:2:0, about 133-134 frames/s were displayed (11-12/s lost) with the wait neutral, versus about 74 at 2000 Mbps / 4:4:4."
  ],
  "decision": ".31 makes the half-frame bounded wait the default (unset property = 4000 us, capped at half a frame; 0 disables). Verified on .31. Ready-FD publication stays opt-in and is not recommended.",
